course: dictionary
lesson: 8464
title: Science words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Science books use special words, and context helps you work out what they mean. «Digestion starts in the mouth with chewing and saliva.» Digestion is what happens to food inside your body, and it starts in the mouth, so saliva must be something the mouth provides to help with food.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Saliva is the clear, watery liquid made in your mouth. Does «Smelling tasty food can make your mouth water» still make sense? Yes, because your mouth makes more saliva, so it feels wet and watery.
explain: Words in «Common vampire bats... lap a little blood, and their saliva stops the blood from clotting» tell you saliva does something to blood. You do not need every detail to understand the main idea. Read on, and come back if you need to.
fact: Saliva is the clear, watery liquid made in your mouth.
fact: Saliva keeps your mouth wet and helps you chew, swallow and start digesting food.
fact: Saliva is uncountable, so you say some saliva, not a saliva.
fact: «Digestion starts in the mouth with chewing and saliva.»
fact: Saliva contains an enzyme that begins breaking down starch, which is why bread can taste sweeter when you chew it for a long time.
exercise: «Digestion starts in the mouth with chewing and saliva.» What is saliva? | *A clear, watery liquid made in your mouth | A kind of food | A type of breath | A hard bone in your jaw
exercise: «Smelling tasty food can make your mouth water.» What does mouth water mean here? | *Your mouth makes more saliva | Your mouth fills with bubbles | Your mouth turns into a drink | Your mouth gets wet from rain
exercise: «Some swiftlets build nests from their own saliva.» What does «their own saliva» tell you? | It is a type of seed | *It comes from the bird's own body | It belongs to the cave | It was found in a shop
test: «Many people are allergic to proteins in pet skin flakes and saliva, which cause sneezing and itchy eyes.» Which words show that saliva can cause trouble? | Many and people | *Allergic and cause | Pet and skin | Sneezing and itchy eyes
test: What does «swallow saliva» mean? | To blow it out through your nose | To wash it off your teeth | To spit it out onto the floor | *To take saliva down your throat
exam: Why does bread taste sweeter when you chew it for a long time? | Bread is made of sugar only | Saliva turns bread into water | *Saliva contains an enzyme that begins breaking down starch | Chewing makes bread warmer
